namespace Lab.Domain.Constants;

public static class Rules
{
    public const string Paper =
        "**A Synthesis of Key Writing Skills for Research Papers**\n\nHere is the comprehensive guide to essential scientific writing skills, formatted in Markdown, to help you structure clear, concise, and impactful research papers.\n\n### 1. Planning and Preparation\n*   **Focus on the reader and referee:** Your ultimate aim is to have your paper published. You will increase your chances of acceptance if referees and readers find your manuscript easy to read and understand. Always think about their expectations.\n*   **Use existing papers as models:** You can significantly improve your English by reading other papers in your field, noting down standard phrases, and using those papers as templates for your own. \n\n### 2. Word Order and Sentence Structure\n*   **Follow basic English word order:** Keep the subject, verb, direct object, and indirect object in a strict sequence and as close to each other as possible.\n*   **Keep the subject and verb together:** The verb contains important information. Inserting parenthetical or extra information between the subject and the verb interrupts the reader's train of thought.\n*   **Write short sentences:** The longer your sentence, the greater the chance it will be misunderstood. A good rule of thumb is to aim for an upper limit of around 25 words. Break up long sentences by using periods instead of stringing clauses together with excessive commas, semicolons, or conjunctions like *and* and *which*.\n\n### 3. Structuring Paragraphs\n*   **Use topic sentences:** Every well-structured paragraph should begin with a topic sentence that tells the reader what the paragraph is about and connects it to the previous paragraph.\n*   **Manage the flow of information:** Place known (old) information at the beginning of a sentence or paragraph to set the context, and push new (and most important) information to the end of the sentence to give it emphasis. Progress logically from general concepts to increasingly specific ones.\n*   **Break up long paragraphs:** Long blocks of text are tiring for the eyes and obscure your key points. Start a new paragraph whenever you change focus or want to introduce a key finding.\n\n### 4. Being Concise and Removing Redundancy\n*   **Write less to make fewer mistakes:** Being concise not only improves readability but also gives you fewer opportunities to make grammatical mistakes. Cut words that add no value.\n*   **Prefer verbs to nouns:** English is more dynamic when using verbs rather than nouns. Instead of using a heavy verb + noun construction (e.g., *make an analysis*, *carry out a test*), use a single action verb (e.g., *analyze*, *test*). \n\n### 5. Avoiding Ambiguity and Vagueness\n*   **Be careful with pronouns:** Pronouns like *it*, *they*, *this*, or *these* frequently cause confusion. To prevent ambiguity, it is always safer to repeat the exact noun rather than using a pronoun.\n*   **Do not use \"the former\" and \"the latter\":** Using these terms forces the reader to read backwards to remember what you are referring to. Simply repeating the exact words is much clearer.\n*   **Avoid \"Monologophobia\" (the fear of repeating words):** Do not constantly search for synonyms for your key words. Using different words to describe the same key concept confuses the reader.\n\n### 6. Clarifying Who Did What\n*   **Distinguish your work from the literature:** A common and serious mistake is failing to make it 100% clear to the referee whose results are being discussed—yours or another author's.\n*   **Use \"we\" or \"our\":** If the journal allows personal forms, use *we* or *our* to clearly differentiate your methodology and findings from previous research. Even if you must use the passive voice, explicitly signal your own work (e.g., *in our study*).\n\n### 7. Highlighting Findings and Hedging\n*   **Highlight your key findings visually:** Make your findings stand out on the page by placing them in a separate, shorter \"key finding paragraph\". Use shorter sentences and dynamic language (e.g., *importantly*, *interestingly*) to draw the reader's eye.\n*   **Hedge your claims:** In Western scientific writing, it is best not to sound 100% certain about subjective interpretations. \"Hedging\" protects you against being proven wrong later. Tone down your verbs (e.g., use *would seem to demonstrate* or *suggests* instead of *proves*) and insert probability adverbs (e.g., *probably*, *possibly*). \n*   **Admit limitations:** Honestly drawing attention to the limitations or flaws in your study does not weaken it; rather, it builds your credibility and portrays you as a reliable, objective researcher.";
}